course: everyday
lesson: 367
title: Wimp, scaredy-cat and other labels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On Tuesday Freddie calls Sam «Scaredy-Sam» as a joke. By lunchtime half the class is saying it. By Wednesday somebody has drawn a chicken on Sam's pencil case in felt tip, and when the cold wind makes his eyes water, Jake yells «Sam's crying!» across the whole playground. Sam does not feel like Sam any more — he feels like a label.
explain: The labels pile up. Sam won't climb the fence into the building site, so Jake calls him a wimp. He won't play British Bulldog (a rough running-and-catching game), so Maddie says «don't be such a scaredy-cat». The class finds out he knits with his gran and decides he is «weird». At the gate Sam even hears two mums say their sons «shouldn't play with that soft lad». That one hurts the most, because grown-ups are meant to be fair and kind.
explain: But Sam is not the only one with a label. Jake gets called «Thicko» because he can't sit still and reads slowly. A Station Road boy calls Maddie «a boy in a skirt», which is funny only because she has never worn a skirt in her life. Some girls sneer «Princess Lily» because her hair is neat and her books are tidy. Each label is one small thing stretched over a whole person.
explain: Sam's gran says: «A label tells you about the person sticking it on, not the person wearing it.» Sam and Gran finish a stripy scarf. In the end Sam gives it to Maddie, who, to everyone's surprise, wears it all winter.
fact: A label is a name that squeezes a whole person into one small thing about them.
fact: A nickname spreads fast when other people repeat it, even if it started as a joke.
fact: Saying no to climbing into a building site is wise: building sites are dangerous and children must stay out.
fact: Calling someone names over and over, or getting others to join in, is a form of bullying.
fact: Wind, cold, dust and onions make eyes water; that is not crying.
fact: Liking a quiet hobby like knitting, reading or drawing does not make anyone weird or weak.
fact: Reading slowly or finding it hard to sit still is not the same as being stupid; many clever people learn differently.
fact: Name-calling often says more about the person doing it than about the person being called names.
fact: Not joining in when a nasty name goes round is a simple way to stop it spreading.
fact: Telling a teacher about names that won't stop is getting help, not being a baby.
exercise: How did «Scaredy-Sam» spread so fast? | Other children kept repeating it | Freddie wrote it on the board | The teacher used it in class | Sam told everyone himself
exercise: Why was Sam right not to climb into the building site? | Building sites are dangerous and off limits | Because the fence was still wet | Because Jake would tell on him | Because he couldn't climb at all
exercise: Sam's eyes watered in the cold wind. Was he crying? | No, cold wind makes eyes water | Yes, watery eyes always mean crying | Yes, because he was sad about Jake | No, he had got soap in his eyes
exercise: Which label hurt Sam the most? | The mums calling him «that soft lad» | The chicken on his pencil case | Maddie saying «scaredy-cat» | Freddie's first joke
exercise: Why did the mums' words hurt more than the children's? | Grown-ups are meant to be fair and kind | Because the mums were very loud | Because they were strangers | Because mums are always right
exercise: Is knitting with his gran a sign that Sam is weird? | No, any hobby is fine for anyone | Yes, knitting is only for grannies | Yes, because boys must never knit | No, because nobody can see him do it
exercise: Why do some children call Jake «Thicko»? | He can't sit still and reads slowly | He failed every test at school | He told them to call him that | He broke a window last year
exercise: What is true about reading slowly? | It doesn't mean someone isn't clever | It means that person can never learn to read well | It only happens to lazy children | It means the person needs glasses
exercise: Why is «a boy in a skirt» a silly name for Maddie? | It doesn't even fit; she never wears skirts | Because skirts are only for boys | Because Maddie is a boy | Because she wears long skirts to school every day
exercise: Why do some girls sneer «Princess Lily»? | Because her hair is neat and her books tidy | Because she lives in a big castle with towers | Because her dad is a king | Because she told them to bow
exercise: What did Gran mean: «A label tells you about the person sticking it on»? | Name-calling shows the caller's own unkindness | Labels on jars tell you exactly what is inside them | Sam should stick labels on others | Labels are always true about people
exercise: What could one classmate do to stop «Scaredy-Sam» spreading? | Refuse to say it and stand by Sam | Say it only on Fridays | Invent an even worse name for Freddie instead | Draw a lion on Sam's pencil case
exercise: How did Sam probably feel after a week of labels? | Like a label instead of a person | Proud and very popular with everyone | Glad that people had noticed him at last | Nothing at all, labels don't matter
exercise: Saying no to British Bulldog — is that a kind of courage? | Yes, saying no when everyone pushes takes nerve | No, saying no to a game is always cowardly | No, games are never a choice | Yes, but only if you cry
exercise: Sam, Jake, Maddie and Lily all got labels. What does that show? | Almost anyone can be stuck with a label | Only weak people ever get labels stuck on them | Labels are only given to boys | Labels are always deserved
exercise: When names won't stop, what is a sensible step? | Tell a teacher and keep friends close | Call them even worse names back so they stop | Stay home from school for good | Change your name by law
test: «Scaredy-Sam lol everyone's saying it». You don't want to. What's best? | Don't say it, and sit with Sam | Say it just once so that you fit in with everyone | Say it but quietly | Make up a new one for Freddie
test: Jake: «you're a wimp, just climb the fence, nobody's on the site». Who's right? | Sam, building sites are dangerous | Jake, the site is empty at night so it's safe | Jake, fences are there for climbing | Nobody, it's only a fence round some mud
test: «look Sam's crying again!!» — it's freezing and windy. What's true? | Cold wind makes anyone's eyes water | He must be crying about Jake again, poor thing | Only weak people's eyes water | It's hay fever in winter
test: «knitting?? with ur nan?? that's well weird mate». Fair? | No, a hobby is a hobby, nothing weird | Yes, knitting is just for old ladies, not for boys | Yes, boys must play football | No, because nobody saw him
test: Teacher, sharp: «Who drew the chicken on Sam's pencil case?» Nobody answers. What's the kind thing for a witness to do? | Tell the teacher quietly afterwards | Keep quiet forever, it's not your business | Draw a chicken on theirs as well | Laugh with the others
test: «Jake's thick, he can't even read» What's true? | Reading slowly isn't the same as being stupid | Jake can never learn anything, so he's thick | Thick people can't sit still | Only lazy children read slowly
test: «oi Maddie, boy in a skirt!» She's in tracksuit bottoms as always. Why's it daft? | It's a name that doesn't even fit her | Because she is wearing a skirt today | Because girls can't wear tracksuits | Because Maddie is really a boy and not a girl
test: «ooh look, Princess Lily's got her tidy pencils out». What's this? | Sneering at her for something good | A kind compliment | A fair warning to her about losing her pencils | A joke Lily asked for
test: Mum at the gate: «my son shouldn't play with that soft lad». Is that a fair way for an adult to talk? | No, it labels a child unkindly | Yes, adults know best about children | Yes, if the boy really does knit | No, but only because Sam heard it
test: Maddie: «don't be such a scaredy-cat, play Bulldog!» Sam doesn't want to. Best reply? | «Nah, not today. I'll play something else» | «Fine, I'll smash you all» | «You're a scaredy-cat yourself, Maddie, not me» | Burst into tears and run home
test: «names never hurt anyone, mate». Which is true? | Names can hurt a lot, especially when they spread | Names can never hurt anyone, only sticks and stones | Names only hurt if they rhyme | Names stop hurting after one day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of a derogatory label to a pupil? | It reduces an entire person to one small characteristic | It provides an accurate summary of character | It is harmless when initiated in jest | It is the most effective manner of correcting behaviour
exam: Identify the principal reason why the pupil Sam was prudent in declining to enter the building site. | Building sites present serious hazards and are prohibited | The fence had been recently painted | His companions were unwilling to accompany him over the fence at that hour | Construction sites are open to the public at weekends
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil who reads slowly and finds it difficult to remain seated. | Such difficulties are no indication of a lack of intelligence | Such a pupil is incapable of any further learning in the ordinary classroom | Such conduct is invariably the result of idleness | Such a pupil ought to be excluded from lessons
exam: Pursuant to the observation that a derogatory nickname is being repeated by numerous pupils, identify the most appropriate conduct of an individual bystander. | To decline to repeat it and to support the pupil concerned | To repeat it so as to remain in favour with the group and avoid ridicule | To devise a further nickname for the originator | To disregard the matter entirely as trivial
